The problem asks us to simulate a process involving $N$ aliens.
Each alien $i$ (1-indexed) starts with $A_i$ stones.
Alien $i$ becomes an adult exactly $i$ years later.
When an alien becomes an adult (at year $i$), every adult who has at least 1 stone gives 1 stone to the newly adult alien.
We need to find the number of stones each alien has after $N$ years.
The constraints are $N \le 5 \times 10^5$ and $A_i \le 5 \times 10^5$.
Since $N$ is up to $5 \times 10^5$, an $O(N^2)$ simulation is too slow. We need something closer to $O(N)$ or $O(N \log N)$.

Let's trace the process.
At year $k$ (where $k$ ranges from 1 to $N$), the $k$-th alien becomes an adult.
The condition is: "every adult who has at least one stone gives exactly one stone".
Who are the adults at year $k$?
The aliens who have become adults are those with index $j$ such that $j \le k$.
Wait, the problem says "The $i$-th alien ... will become an adult exactly $i$ years later."
So at year 1, alien 1 becomes an adult.
At year 2, alien 2 becomes an adult.
...
At year $k$, alien $k$ becomes an adult.
The set of adults at year $k$ (just before the event for alien $k$ happens) includes aliens $1, 2, \dots, k-1$.
Wait, the phrasing "every adult who has at least one stone gives exactly one stone as a congratulatory gift to the alien who has just become an adult" implies that the aliens who are already adults participate.
Does the newly adult alien participate in giving stones? No, they just receive.
So, at year $k$, alien $k$ receives stones from all aliens $j < k$ who are currently adults and have $\ge 1$ stone.
Wait, let's check the sample explanation.
Sample 1:
$N=4$, $A = [5, 0, 9, 3]$.
Initially: $C_1=5, C_2=0, C_3=9, C_4=3$.
After 1 year: Alien 1 becomes adult.
Adults before this event: None. So Alien 1 receives 0 stones.
State: $C_1=5, C_2=0, C_3=9, C_4=3$.
Wait, the sample explanation says:
"After 1 year, (C_1, C_2, C_3, C_4) = (5, 0, 9, 3)."
This matches. Alien 1 is now an adult.
"After 2 years, (C_1, C_2, C_3, C_4) = (4, 1, 9, 3)."
Alien 2 becomes adult. Adults are {1}.
Alien 1 has 5 stones ($\ge 1$). So Alien 1 gives 1 stone to Alien 2.
Alien 1 has $5-1=4$. Alien 2 has $0+1=1$.
State: $C_1=4, C_2=1, C_3=9, C_4=3$.
"After 3 years, (C_1, C_2, C_3, C_4) = (3, 0, 11, 3)."
Alien 3 becomes adult. Adults are {1, 2}.
Alien 1 has 4 stones ($\ge 1$). Gives 1.
Alien 2 has 1 stone ($\ge 1$). Gives 1.
Alien 1 becomes $4-1=3$.
Alien 2 becomes $1-1=0$.
Alien 3 becomes $9+2=11$.
State: $C_1=3, C_2=0, C_3=11, C_4=3$.
"After 4 years, (C_1, C_2, C_3, C_4) = (2, 0, 10, 5)."
